\f3\b \cb7 They enhance security by concealing model logic
\f1\b0 \'a0- Opaque models, not transparent ones, are typically associated with enhanced security through obscurity. Transparent models, by definition, reveal their internal workings, which can be less secure if the logic itself needs to be protected.\cb1 \

\f3\b \cb7 They simplify the integration process with other systems
\f1\b0 \'a0- The ease of integrating a model with other systems is more related to the architecture and compatibility of the model with existing systems rather than its transparency. Transparent models do not inherently simplify integration processes.\cb1 \
