\f3\b \cf2 \cb7 Self-supervised learning
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 It works when models are provided vast amounts of raw, almost entirely, or completely unlabeled data and then generate the labels themselves.\cb1 \
\cb7 Foundation models use self-supervised learning to create labels from input data. In self-supervised learning, models are provided vast amounts of raw completely unlabeled data and then the models generate the labels themselves. This means no one has instructed or trained the model with labeled training data sets.\cb1 \
\cb7 Incorrect options:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb7 Reinforcement learning
\f1\b0 \'a0- Reinforcement learning is a method with reward values attached to the different steps that the algorithm must go through. So the model\'92s goal is to accumulate as many reward points as possible and eventually reach an end goal.\cb1 \

\f3\b \cb7 Supervised learning
\f1\b0 \'a0- In supervised learning, models are supplied with labeled and defined training data to assess for correlations. The sample data specifies both the input and the output for the model. For example, images of handwritten figures are annotated to indicate which number they correspond to. A supervised learning system could recognize the clusters of pixels and shapes associated with each number, given sufficient examples.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 Data labeling is the process of categorizing input data with its corresponding defined output values. Labeled training data is required for supervised learning. For example, millions of apple and banana images would need to be tagged with the words \'93apple\'94 or \'93banana.\'94 Then machine learning applications could use this training data to guess the name of the fruit when given a fruit image.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb7 Unsupervised learning
\f1\b0 \'a0- Unsupervised learning algorithms train on unlabeled data. They scan through new data, trying to establish meaningful connections between the inputs and predetermined outputs. They can spot patterns and categorize data. For example, unsupervised algorithms could group news articles from different news sites into common categories like sports, crime, etc. They can use natural language processing to comprehend meaning and emotion in the article.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
