\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb7 Foundation models can perform a wide range of tasks across different domains by leveraging their extensive pre-training on large datasets
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 Foundation models are a form of generative artificial intelligence (generative AI). They generate output from one or more inputs (prompts) in the form of human language instructions.\cb1 \
\cb7 In general, an FM uses learned patterns and relationships to predict the next item in a sequence. For example, with image generation, the model analyzes the image and creates a sharper, more clearly defined version of the image. Similarly, with text, the model predicts the next word in a string of text based on the previous words and their context. It then selects the next word using probability distribution techniques.\cb1 \
\cb7 Foundation models use self-supervised learning to create labels from input data. This means no one has instructed or trained the model with labeled training data sets. This feature separates LLMs from previous ML architectures, which use supervised or unsupervised learning.\cb1 \
\cb7 Foundation models, even though are pre-trained, can continue to learn from data inputs or prompts during inference. This means that you can develop comprehensive outputs through carefully curated prompts. Tasks that FMs can perform include language processing, visual comprehension, code generation, and human-centered engagement.\cb1 \
