\f1 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 An Internet-of-Things (IoT) company is developing a suite of smart sensors and devices that rely on real-time data processing to enable applications like predictive maintenance, environmental monitoring, and immediate anomaly detection. To provide immediate feedback and actions, the company needs to deploy machine learning models directly on its edge devices, ensuring that these models can perform inference with minimal latency. The company is evaluating different approaches to optimize performance and maintain low-latency inference on these edge devices.\cb1 \
\cb3 Which approach would be the most suitable for meeting this requirement?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38\fsmilli19200 \cf6 \cb7 \strokec6 The company should use an optimized large language model (LLM) deployed directly on the edge device, allowing for real-time, low-latency inference\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38\fsmilli19200 \cf6 \cb7 \strokec6 The company should use a central API connected to a small language model (SLM) with an asynchronous inference endpoint, which allows the model to handle requests from multiple edge devices\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \strokec5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38\fsmilli19200 \cf6 \cb7 \strokec6 The company should use a central API connected to a large language model (LLM) with an asynchronous inference endpoint, which allows the model to handle requests from multiple edge devices\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs32 \cf2 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf8 \strokec8 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38\fsmilli19200 \cf2 \cb12 \strokec2 The company should use an optimized small language model (SLM) deployed directly on the edge device, allowing for real-time, low-latency inference\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 Overall explanation
\f1 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 Correct option:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb7 The company should use an optimized small language model (SLM) deployed directly on the edge device, allowing for real-time, low-latency inference
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 This is the most suitable choice because a small language model (SLM) optimized for deployment on edge devices is specifically designed to be lightweight, efficient, and capable of running on devices with limited computational resources. Deploying the model directly on the edge device eliminates the need for network communication with a central server, thereby achieving the required low-latency inference needed for real-time IoT applications.\cb1 \
\cb7 Incorrect options:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf2 \cb7 The company should use an optimized large language model (LLM) deployed directly on the edge device, allowing for real-time, low-latency inference
\f1\b0 \'a0- While deploying a model on the edge device is beneficial for reducing latency, a large language model (LLM) is generally too resource-intensive for edge devices, which often have limited memory, processing power, and storage. LLMs typically require substantial computational resources that exceed the capabilities of most edge devices, leading to poor performance or the inability to run at all.\cb1 \

\f3\b \cb7 The company should use a central API connected to a large language model (LLM) with an asynchronous inference endpoint, which allows the model to handle requests from multiple edge devices
\f1\b0 \'a0- This option is less suitable because relying on a central API and asynchronous inference endpoint introduces network latency, which contradicts the requirement for low-latency inference. Additionally, LLMs are computationally heavy and may not provide the timely responses needed for real-time IoT applications when accessed over a network.\cb1 \

\f3\b \cb7 The company should use a central API connected to a small language model (SLM) with an asynchronous inference endpoint, which allows the model to handle requests from multiple edge devices
\f1\b0 \'a0- Although a small language model (SLM) is more lightweight and efficient than an LLM, using a central API with asynchronous inference endpoints still involves network communication that can result in latency. This approach does not meet the low-latency requirement needed for edge-based real-time processing, as network delays could adversely affect performance.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
